  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 200 AM MDT Sat Sep 5 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nA deep and slow moving upper level trough over the Eastern \nPacific/Western US will slowly move eastward over the coming days.\nAs it moves eastward, expect rain along the Continental Divide \ntoday to slowly shift eastward and expand, with more widespread \nshowers and thunderstorms Sunday, Monday, and early Tuesday before\nthis system departs late Tuesday morning and Wednesday. Behind \nthis system, drier weather is expected Wednesday and Thursday as \nweak upper level ridging transits the area, which will also bring \ntemperatures back closer to average after a cool start to the \nweek. However, more unsettled weather looks likely to close out \nthe week as yet another upper level trough pushes into the Western\nUS. \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThunderstorms Sunday: Recent Hi Res model guidance has begun to \nhint at the possibility of a round of thunderstorms on Sunday \ncapable of producing some strong to severe wind gusts. While it \ndoes not currently look quite as damaging as Wednesday\u2019s event \nwas, there are still a few things to note about the severe threat.\nModel soundings and hodographs (temperature/humidity profiles and\nwind profiles, respectively) show quite a bit of instability in \nthe mid/upper levels, along with ample shear to help storms form \nand then organize and strengthen as they race northeastward thanks\nto relatively strong winds (in excess of 50 knots/60 mph). Given \nthe wet environment, it will be difficult for any larger hail to \nreach the surface, but some smaller hail may still reach the \nsurface. Winds are a bit tricky as well, as while there is both \nsufficient dry air near the surface and winds aloft to result in \nsome strong wind gusts, there are also hints of some capping which\nmay limit transport of the winds to the surface. Given the \nuncertainty, SPC has placed much of North Central and Central \nMontana on Sunday, which seems reasonable at the moment given the \nuncertainty. \n\nRainfall through Tuesday: Models remain fairly consistent on a \nperiod of widespread soaking rainfall across North Central and \nSouthwestern Montana through Tuesday. Most models also remain \nconsistent in placing the heaviest precipitation north of a Helena \nto Great Falls to Lewistown Line, where amounts of 1to 2 inches \nappear likely. However, there remains some possibility that some \nareas could see more significant rainfall totals (in excess of 3 or \n4 inches), which could result in some increases to rivers and \nstreams. Widespread flooding remains unlikely, though some areas \ncould see some ponding of poor drainage areas. Ludwig",
